1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:01,860
Summer, 1898.

2
00:00:01,860 --> 00:00:03,184
You are a volunteer.

3
00:00:03,184 --> 00:00:05,235
Weeks ago you had another life.

4
00:00:05,235 --> 00:00:08,676
Now you are standing in a field of white tents, in a

5
00:00:08,676 --> 00:00:10,397
camp that is already full.

6
00:00:10,847 --> 00:00:12,269
You came to fight Spain.

7
00:00:12,269 --> 00:00:13,276
You have a rifle.

8
00:00:13,276 --> 00:00:16,357
But the enemy that is coming for you is not in Cuba.

9
00:00:16,357 --> 00:00:18,134
It is already inside the camp.

10
00:00:18,584 --> 00:00:19,613
It is in the water.

11
00:00:19,613 --> 00:00:20,642
It is on the flies.

12
00:00:20,642 --> 00:00:22,863
It is on the hands of the men around you.

13
00:00:23,313 --> 00:00:25,629
Here is that war in two numbers.

14
00:00:25,629 --> 00:00:29,827
In the Spanish–American War, 385 Americans died in battle.

15
00:00:29,827 --> 00:00:33,881
That is the count of the Congressional Research Service.

16
00:00:34,331 --> 00:00:37,355
And 2,061 died of other causes.

17
00:00:37,805 --> 00:00:41,089
The general commanding the Fifth Army Corps, William

18
00:00:41,089 --> 00:00:44,057
Shafter, said that disease was a thousand times

19
00:00:44,057 --> 00:00:47,720
harder to stand up against than the missiles of the enemy.

20
00:00:48,170 --> 00:00:52,045
Typhoid fever alone, in the training camps, sickened

21
00:00:52,045 --> 00:00:53,610
more than 20,000 men.

22
00:00:53,610 --> 00:00:55,174
More than 1,500 died.

23
00:00:55,624 --> 00:00:57,756
And it was not a one-off.

24
00:00:57,756 --> 00:01:00,571
It is a pattern in American wars.

25
00:01:00,571 --> 00:01:05,517
In the Mexican War, 11,550 Americans died of other causes.

26
00:01:05,517 --> 00:01:07,308
1,733 died in battle.

27
00:01:07,758 --> 00:01:13,749
In the Civil War, on the Union side, 224,097.

28
00:01:13,749 --> 00:01:17,210
Against 140,414 in battle.

29
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:23,067
In the World War I, 63,114 other deaths.

30
00:01:23,067 --> 00:01:25,365
53,402 in battle.

31
00:01:25,815 --> 00:01:28,396
And camps like these would fill again.

32
00:01:28,396 --> 00:01:31,860
Twenty years later, draftees would arrive at a camp

33
00:01:31,860 --> 00:01:34,916
in Ohio, to be examined, inoculated, and fed.

34
00:01:35,366 --> 00:01:38,851
Other deaths includes accidents, and the old records

35
00:01:38,851 --> 00:01:39,856
are incomplete.

36
00:01:39,856 --> 00:01:43,072
But the military's National Museum of Health and

37
00:01:43,072 --> 00:01:46,088
Medicine says it plainly about the Civil War:

38
00:01:46,088 --> 00:01:49,640
for every life lost to a bullet, disease claimed two.

39
00:01:50,090 --> 00:01:51,873
The rule broke in World War II.

40
00:01:51,873 --> 00:01:54,865
There, battle killed far more American soldiers than

41
00:01:54,865 --> 00:01:55,555
disease did.

42
00:01:55,555 --> 00:01:57,741
Disease filled the sick lists instead.

43
00:01:57,741 --> 00:01:58,892
We will get to that.

44
00:01:59,342 --> 00:02:02,469
So this is the story of the enemy inside the camp,

45
00:02:02,469 --> 00:02:05,534
and where the title holds, and where it does not.

46
00:02:05,534 --> 00:02:07,973
And how medicine learned to fight back.

47
00:02:10,373 --> 00:02:13,098
It started with a summer, and an army that crowded

48
00:02:13,098 --> 00:02:13,698
into camps.

49
00:02:15,298 --> 00:02:18,642
In 1898, the United States went to war with Spain.

50
00:02:18,642 --> 00:02:22,521
Volunteer regiments poured toward the camps and the ports.

51
00:02:22,971 --> 00:02:25,695
The military's National Museum of Health and

52
00:02:25,695 --> 00:02:28,233
Medicine says typhoid swept through those

53
00:02:28,233 --> 00:02:31,824
overcrowded camps, and caused far more deaths than combat.

54
00:02:31,824 --> 00:02:33,681
About 1 in 5 troops caught it.

55
00:02:34,131 --> 00:02:36,965
Typhoid is a bacterial infection that spreads

56
00:02:36,965 --> 00:02:39,170
through contaminated food or water.

57
00:02:39,170 --> 00:02:42,256
A camp of tens of thousands of men, with latrines

58
00:02:42,256 --> 00:02:45,594
and kitchens and flies, gave it everything it needed.

59
00:02:46,044 --> 00:02:49,226
One of the biggest was Camp Thomas, at Chickamauga.

60
00:02:49,226 --> 00:02:51,972
More than 45,000 volunteers were sent there.

61
00:02:52,422 --> 00:02:55,926
In August, the Army sent Major Walter Reed to Chickamauga.

62
00:02:55,926 --> 00:02:58,706
With two colleagues, Victor Vaughan and Edward

63
00:02:58,706 --> 00:03:02,151
Shakespeare, he would write the Army's report on typhoid.

64
00:03:02,601 --> 00:03:04,549
What Reed saw was not mysterious.

65
00:03:04,549 --> 00:03:07,501
In accounts of his visit, the latrines were filled

66
00:03:07,501 --> 00:03:10,335
to the top, and flies moved between them and the

67
00:03:10,335 --> 00:03:10,985
men's food.

68
00:03:11,435 --> 00:03:14,471
The Board's report describes flies as one way the

69
00:03:14,471 --> 00:03:15,400
disease spread.

70
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:18,251
Decades later, the Army was still teaching the

71
00:03:18,251 --> 00:03:21,163
lesson about flies, in a training cartoon about

72
00:03:21,163 --> 00:03:24,075
dysentery, with flies crawling over a mess kit.

73
00:03:24,525 --> 00:03:26,954
Then the Board did something new.

74
00:03:26,954 --> 00:03:30,561
It studied the medical histories of 92 regiments.

75
00:03:30,561 --> 00:03:32,180
More than 100,000 men.

76
00:03:32,630 --> 00:03:36,162
The result ran to nearly 3,000 manuscript pages,

77
00:03:36,162 --> 00:03:38,001
with 100 maps and charts.

78
00:03:38,001 --> 00:03:40,429
Many of them were plans of camps.

79
00:03:40,429 --> 00:03:41,606
Each box a tent.

80
00:03:41,606 --> 00:03:42,856
Each mark a case.

81
00:03:43,306 --> 00:03:44,690
The method was simple.

82
00:03:44,690 --> 00:03:45,696
Count the cases.

83
00:03:45,696 --> 00:03:46,702
Mark every tent.

84
00:03:46,702 --> 00:03:48,525
Find the sinks and the wells.

85
00:03:48,525 --> 00:03:51,229
Then ask what the sick tents had in common.

86
00:03:51,679 --> 00:03:53,660
The Board found something strange.

87
00:03:53,660 --> 00:03:56,632
Some tents were badly infected, while a tent beside

88
00:03:56,632 --> 00:03:59,370
them, sharing the same water and food, escaped.

89
00:03:59,820 --> 00:04:01,323
The answer was people.

90
00:04:01,323 --> 00:04:04,603
Men carried it with them from tent to tent, even

91
00:04:04,603 --> 00:04:06,994
some who never fell ill themselves.

92
00:04:06,994 --> 00:04:10,273
A summary of the report says man himself was the

93
00:04:10,273 --> 00:04:12,596
most active agent in spreading it.

94
00:04:13,046 --> 00:04:18,885
At Camp Thomas alone, more than 10,000 men caught typhoid.

95
00:04:18,885 --> 00:04:19,791
761 died.

96
00:04:19,791 --> 00:04:24,120
That was 48% of the Board's count of 1,580.

97
00:04:24,570 --> 00:04:27,372
The Board's abstract came out in 1900.

98
00:04:27,372 --> 00:04:29,806
The full report followed in 1904.

99
00:04:29,806 --> 00:04:33,568
Each set out 57 general statements and conclusions.

100
00:04:34,018 --> 00:04:35,553
Datasets differ.

101
00:04:35,553 --> 00:04:40,734
The CRS death count covers only May to August of 1898.

102
00:04:40,734 --> 00:04:45,627
The Army's later history lists 20,926 typhoid cases

103
00:04:45,627 --> 00:04:49,081
and 2,192 deaths, for the whole war.

104
00:04:49,531 --> 00:04:52,277
And typhoid was not the only danger.

105
00:04:52,277 --> 00:04:56,014
In the campaign in Cuba, PBS says more than 2,000

106
00:04:56,014 --> 00:04:58,073
troops caught yellow fever.

107
00:04:58,073 --> 00:04:59,828
That story comes later.

108
00:05:00,278 --> 00:05:02,027
The Board's lesson was clear.

109
00:05:02,027 --> 00:05:05,165
Typhoid moved from man to man, by contact, flies and

110
00:05:05,165 --> 00:05:05,527
water.

111
00:05:05,527 --> 00:05:08,604
Camps could be laid out and run so that it had less

112
00:05:08,604 --> 00:05:09,389
to work with.

113
00:05:09,839 --> 00:05:12,499
And the Army learned a second lesson.

114
00:05:12,499 --> 00:05:16,381
In 1909, it offered typhoid vaccination to volunteers.

115
00:05:16,381 --> 00:05:18,107
In 1911, it required it.

116
00:05:18,557 --> 00:05:24,306
In 1911, a vaccinated division of 12,800 men, in San

117
00:05:24,306 --> 00:05:28,839
Antonio, had one typhoid case, no deaths.

118
00:05:28,839 --> 00:05:33,814
In 1898, a division of 10,700 had 248 deaths.

119
00:05:34,264 --> 00:05:38,282
In 1917, draftees arrived at camps like this one in Ohio.

120
00:05:38,282 --> 00:05:41,666
The film shows them lined up, examined, and fed.

121
00:05:42,116 --> 00:05:45,007
The camp had its own bakery, its own kitchens, and

122
00:05:45,007 --> 00:05:45,932
its own laundry.

123
00:05:46,382 --> 00:05:50,096
By the World War I, the Army counted 1,529 primary

124
00:05:50,096 --> 00:05:51,507
typhoid admissions.

125
00:05:51,507 --> 00:05:55,370
In the Spanish–American War, the yearly typhoid rate

126
00:05:55,370 --> 00:05:59,306
had been more than 300 times higher per thousand men.

127
00:05:59,756 --> 00:06:02,735
The full story of that vaccine comes later.

128
00:06:02,735 --> 00:06:05,160
But the idea behind it was not new.

129
00:06:05,160 --> 00:06:08,486
A general had faced the same choice, more than a

130
00:06:08,486 --> 00:06:09,525
century before.

131
00:06:09,975 --> 00:06:14,066
It started with a letter, on February 6, 1777.

132
00:06:15,666 --> 00:06:18,881
The Continental Army had an enemy that no musket

133
00:06:18,881 --> 00:06:20,287
could stop. Smallpox.

134
00:06:20,287 --> 00:06:23,100
About 3 in 10 soldiers who caught it died.

135
00:06:23,550 --> 00:06:27,408
According to the National Museum of Health and

136
00:06:27,408 --> 00:06:31,601
Medicine, in one case in 1776, 5,000 out of 10,000

137
00:06:31,601 --> 00:06:35,039
Continental soldiers contracted smallpox.

138
00:06:35,039 --> 00:06:36,716
It forced a retreat.

139
00:06:37,166 --> 00:06:41,177
In February 1776, General Arnold banned soldiers

140
00:06:41,177 --> 00:06:43,516
from inoculating themselves.

141
00:06:43,516 --> 00:06:47,777
In Canada, General John Thomas refused inoculation,

142
00:06:47,777 --> 00:06:50,785
caught smallpox, and died that year.

143
00:06:51,235 --> 00:06:54,255
Inoculation meant catching smallpox on purpose.

144
00:06:54,255 --> 00:06:57,275
A doctor put matter from a smallpox sore into a

145
00:06:57,275 --> 00:06:58,688
small cut in the skin.

146
00:06:58,688 --> 00:07:01,579
The idea was to survive a milder case, and be

147
00:07:01,579 --> 00:07:02,864
protected afterward.

148
00:07:03,314 --> 00:07:05,945
It worked, but it was dangerous.

149
00:07:05,945 --> 00:07:07,754
In Boston in 1721, Dr.

150
00:07:07,754 --> 00:07:12,029
Boylston estimated that natural smallpox killed 14%,

151
00:07:12,029 --> 00:07:13,674
and inoculation two.

152
00:07:14,124 --> 00:07:15,119
14% against 2.

153
00:07:15,119 --> 00:07:18,603
Those are estimates, and they came from one city.

154
00:07:19,053 --> 00:07:22,206
Even so, inoculation carried a risk of death.

155
00:07:22,206 --> 00:07:25,778
The National Library of Medicine calls Washington's

156
00:07:25,778 --> 00:07:27,740
decision bold and dangerous.

157
00:07:28,190 --> 00:07:29,577
Think about the choice.

158
00:07:29,577 --> 00:07:32,532
To protect his army from smallpox, he had to give

159
00:07:32,532 --> 00:07:34,583
his soldiers smallpox. On purpose.

160
00:07:34,583 --> 00:07:36,935
With a procedure that could still kill.

161
00:07:37,385 --> 00:07:39,378
He told Congress on February 5.

162
00:07:39,378 --> 00:07:41,243
The next day, he wrote to Dr.

163
00:07:41,243 --> 00:07:42,529
William Shippen Jr..

164
00:07:42,529 --> 00:07:44,329
One sentence set the policy.

165
00:07:44,779 --> 00:07:46,107
He gave his reason.

166
00:07:46,107 --> 00:07:49,600
Necessity, he wrote, not only authorizes but seems

167
00:07:49,600 --> 00:07:51,207
to require the measure.

168
00:07:51,657 --> 00:07:54,786
To him, smallpox was more to dread than the sword of

169
00:07:54,786 --> 00:07:55,388
the enemy.

170
00:07:55,838 --> 00:07:59,613
The Military Health System estimates that about 90%

171
00:07:59,613 --> 00:08:03,831
of Continental Army deaths came from disease, not combat.

172
00:08:04,281 --> 00:08:06,424
How bad was disease in that war?

173
00:08:06,424 --> 00:08:09,840
One study, cited by Mount Vernon, suggests that for

174
00:08:09,840 --> 00:08:13,188
every soldier who fell to the British, ten died of

175
00:08:13,188 --> 00:08:14,595
some kind of disease.

176
00:08:15,045 --> 00:08:17,243
Mount Vernon adds a warning.

177
00:08:17,243 --> 00:08:20,933
It is difficult to track smallpox deaths in the

178
00:08:20,933 --> 00:08:22,346
Revolutionary War.

179
00:08:22,346 --> 00:08:25,329
The epidemic lasted from 1775 to 1782.

180
00:08:25,779 --> 00:08:28,846
The Military Health System says less than a quarter

181
00:08:28,846 --> 00:08:31,913
of American colonial troops had ever had the virus.

182
00:08:31,913 --> 00:08:34,438
Most of the army had no protection at all.

183
00:08:34,888 --> 00:08:38,535
According to the National Park Service, by late 1777

184
00:08:38,535 --> 00:08:41,621
inoculation was established in the Army, and

185
00:08:41,621 --> 00:08:44,356
smallpox prevalence fell substantially.

186
00:08:44,806 --> 00:08:48,296
Historians still weigh how much it changed the war.

187
00:08:48,296 --> 00:08:50,623
The order itself is on the record.

188
00:08:50,623 --> 00:08:54,181
It was an order about disease, in an army where, one

189
00:08:54,181 --> 00:08:57,740
study suggests, illness killed far more men than the

190
00:08:57,740 --> 00:08:58,287
British.

191
00:08:58,737 --> 00:09:00,815
That order went after one disease.

192
00:09:00,815 --> 00:09:02,648
It did nothing about the rest.

193
00:09:02,648 --> 00:09:05,703
And the next time America sent an army abroad, the

194
00:09:05,703 --> 00:09:06,986
pattern would repeat.

195
00:09:07,436 --> 00:09:10,284
It started with a war most Americans have forgotten.

196
00:09:11,884 --> 00:09:15,170
In 1846, the United States sent armies into Mexico.

197
00:09:15,170 --> 00:09:17,554
One fought its way through the north.

198
00:09:17,554 --> 00:09:20,904
Another landed on the Gulf coast and marched inland.

199
00:09:21,354 --> 00:09:24,170
At Monterrey, American troops stormed a city that

200
00:09:24,170 --> 00:09:26,008
had been turned into a fortress.

201
00:09:26,458 --> 00:09:30,486
At Veracruz, in March 1847, General Winfield Scott

202
00:09:30,486 --> 00:09:32,339
laid siege to the port.

203
00:09:32,789 --> 00:09:35,592
Engravings of the day show Monterrey's Bishop's

204
00:09:35,592 --> 00:09:38,693
Palace, one of the places the battle was fought for.

205
00:09:39,143 --> 00:09:42,665
Those are the battles in the history books. Buena Vista.

206
00:09:42,665 --> 00:09:43,420
Chapultepec.

207
00:09:43,420 --> 00:09:44,929
The fall of Mexico City.

208
00:09:45,379 --> 00:09:47,148
So here is the other count.

209
00:09:47,148 --> 00:09:49,310
The one nobody put on a monument.

210
00:09:49,760 --> 00:09:53,340
The Congressional Research Service lists 1,733

211
00:09:53,340 --> 00:09:56,608
American battle deaths in the Mexican War.

212
00:09:57,058 --> 00:10:02,464
Then it lists a second line. Other deaths. 11,550.

213
00:10:02,914 --> 00:10:05,443
Total American deaths: 13,283.

214
00:10:05,443 --> 00:10:08,984
Nearly nine in ten were not battle deaths.

215
00:10:09,434 --> 00:10:12,541
The Department of Veterans Affairs prints the same

216
00:10:12,541 --> 00:10:15,523
two figures in its fact sheet on America's wars.

217
00:10:15,523 --> 00:10:16,765
One line for battle.

218
00:10:16,765 --> 00:10:18,567
One line for everything else.

219
00:10:19,017 --> 00:10:20,292
Do the arithmetic.

220
00:10:20,292 --> 00:10:23,692
For every man who died in battle, close to seven

221
00:10:23,692 --> 00:10:25,109
died some other way.

222
00:10:25,559 --> 00:10:27,609
Be careful with that second line.

223
00:10:27,609 --> 00:10:29,598
Other deaths is not a diagnosis.

224
00:10:29,598 --> 00:10:32,829
The table does not say what each man died of, and it

225
00:10:32,829 --> 00:10:34,196
counts only Americans.

226
00:10:34,646 --> 00:10:37,989
The figures come from the Commissioner of Pensions,

227
00:10:37,989 --> 00:10:40,742
in his annual report for fiscal year 1903.

228
00:10:40,742 --> 00:10:42,578
Decades after the war ended.

229
00:10:43,028 --> 00:10:45,135
And the report adds a warning.

230
00:10:45,135 --> 00:10:48,436
Its numbers for wars before the World War I are

231
00:10:48,436 --> 00:10:51,385
based on incomplete records in many cases.

232
00:10:51,835 --> 00:10:54,335
But even with that doubt, the shape is clear.

233
00:10:54,335 --> 00:10:57,225
In this war, an army lost far more men away from the

234
00:10:57,225 --> 00:10:58,169
fight than in it.

235
00:10:58,619 --> 00:11:00,847
Americans did not call it a lesson.

236
00:11:00,847 --> 00:11:02,438
They called it a victory.

237
00:11:02,438 --> 00:11:05,685
They went home, and the numbers went into a pension

238
00:11:05,685 --> 00:11:06,130
report.

239
00:11:06,580 --> 00:11:09,709
In 1861, the country would raise armies again.

240
00:11:09,709 --> 00:11:10,798
Far bigger ones.

241
00:11:10,798 --> 00:11:13,927
And this time, someone would count everything.

242
00:11:14,377 --> 00:11:16,821
It started with farm boys, packed into camps.

243
00:11:18,421 --> 00:11:22,009
In 1861, thousands of young men left small towns and

244
00:11:22,009 --> 00:11:24,009
farms to join the Union army.

245
00:11:24,009 --> 00:11:26,907
They gathered in camps outside Washington.

246
00:11:27,357 --> 00:11:30,058
Camps sprang up on the hills around Washington.

247
00:11:30,058 --> 00:11:32,587
Row on row of tents, and every man in them a

248
00:11:32,587 --> 00:11:33,794
stranger to the next.

249
00:11:34,244 --> 00:11:37,481
Many had never been exposed to the common childhood

250
00:11:37,481 --> 00:11:39,068
diseases. Measles. Mumps.

251
00:11:39,068 --> 00:11:42,051
The National Museum of Health and Medicine says

252
00:11:42,051 --> 00:11:44,908
rural recruits like these often died of them.

253
00:11:45,358 --> 00:11:49,466
The American Battlefield Trust counts 67,000 Union

254
00:11:49,466 --> 00:11:51,273
soldiers with measles.

255
00:11:51,273 --> 00:11:52,999
More than 4,000 died.

256
00:11:53,449 --> 00:11:56,706
The National Museum of Health and Medicine sums up

257
00:11:56,706 --> 00:11:58,661
the whole war in one sentence.

258
00:11:58,661 --> 00:12:02,114
For every life lost to a bullet, disease claimed two.

259
00:12:02,564 --> 00:12:06,019
Soldiers also sickened with smallpox.

260
00:12:06,019 --> 00:12:10,783
The Battlefields Trust counts 12,236 recorded cases

261
00:12:10,783 --> 00:12:15,079
in the Union army, from May 1861 to June 1866.

262
00:12:15,529 --> 00:12:17,105
Now the whole war.

263
00:12:17,105 --> 00:12:21,308
The Congressional Research Service lists 140,414

264
00:12:21,308 --> 00:12:23,059
Union battle deaths.

265
00:12:23,509 --> 00:12:25,739
And 224,097 other deaths.

266
00:12:25,739 --> 00:12:30,378
More than half of all Union deaths did not come from

267
00:12:30,378 --> 00:12:31,002
battle.

268
00:12:31,452 --> 00:12:34,704
The National Park Service puts it more bluntly.

269
00:12:34,704 --> 00:12:38,163
Twice as many Civil War soldiers died from disease

270
00:12:38,163 --> 00:12:39,685
as from battle wounds.

271
00:12:40,135 --> 00:12:42,304
Why two to one, and not one and a half?

272
00:12:42,304 --> 00:12:45,140
The two counts come from different tables, and they

273
00:12:45,140 --> 00:12:47,755
do not define their categories in the same way.

274
00:12:48,205 --> 00:12:51,372
So the title of this video holds for the Civil War.

275
00:12:51,372 --> 00:12:54,602
More Union men died of causes other than battle than

276
00:12:54,602 --> 00:12:55,534
died in battle.

277
00:12:55,984 --> 00:12:58,231
Either way, the direction does not change.

278
00:12:58,231 --> 00:13:00,907
For the Union army, the camp was deadlier than the

279
00:13:00,907 --> 00:13:01,549
battlefield.

280
00:13:01,999 --> 00:13:05,071
Think about what that means in a single regiment.

281
00:13:05,071 --> 00:13:08,331
If the average man fell sick twice a year, then in a

282
00:13:08,331 --> 00:13:11,654
camp of a thousand, the sick call never really ended.

283
00:13:12,104 --> 00:13:14,531
The Army counted the sicknesses, too.

284
00:13:14,531 --> 00:13:17,484
Union surgeons reported over 6 million cases,

285
00:13:17,484 --> 00:13:20,108
roughly two for every soldier each year.

286
00:13:20,558 --> 00:13:23,804
Dysentery alone made up one quarter of all reported

287
00:13:23,804 --> 00:13:24,504
sicknesses.

288
00:13:24,954 --> 00:13:27,988
Here is what the Army's own tables list, from the

289
00:13:27,988 --> 00:13:31,517
Medical and Surgical History of the War of the Rebellion.

290
00:13:31,517 --> 00:13:32,941
Diarrhea and dysentery:

291
00:13:32,941 --> 00:13:36,099
more than one million seven hundred thousand cases,

292
00:13:36,099 --> 00:13:39,318
forty-four thousand five hundred fifty-eight deaths.

293
00:13:39,768 --> 00:13:42,565
The surgeons who filled in those reports were not

294
00:13:42,565 --> 00:13:43,821
writing for headlines.

295
00:13:43,821 --> 00:13:45,705
They were writing for the record.

296
00:13:45,705 --> 00:13:48,674
After the war, the Surgeon General's office gathered

297
00:13:48,674 --> 00:13:50,615
them into a vast official history.

298
00:13:51,065 --> 00:13:54,645
Typhoid, 34,833 deaths in that table.

299
00:13:54,645 --> 00:13:59,387
The National Museum of Civil War Medicine gives a

300
00:13:59,387 --> 00:14:01,903
lower count, about 27,000.

301
00:14:01,903 --> 00:14:03,548
Sources disagree.

302
00:14:03,548 --> 00:14:05,677
Every version is grim.

303
00:14:06,127 --> 00:14:09,362
Malaria, 10,063 deaths. Typhus, 958.

304
00:14:09,362 --> 00:14:13,945
And pneumonia, the museum says, killed 20,000 Union

305
00:14:13,945 --> 00:14:14,574
troops.

306
00:14:15,024 --> 00:14:19,083
Yellow fever shows up in the same tables, with 436 deaths.

307
00:14:19,083 --> 00:14:22,723
A smaller number, but one more thing a camp could do

308
00:14:22,723 --> 00:14:23,353
to a man.

309
00:14:23,803 --> 00:14:26,402
Now be fair to the title of this video.

310
00:14:26,402 --> 00:14:29,867
No single disease in that table killed more than the

311
00:14:29,867 --> 00:14:31,667
140,000 who died in battle.

312
00:14:32,117 --> 00:14:33,036
It was the sum.

313
00:14:33,036 --> 00:14:35,916
Add only those four lines, and you pass 90,000.

314
00:14:35,916 --> 00:14:38,979
Then add everything else a camp could do to a man.

315
00:14:39,429 --> 00:14:42,615
Today the Civil War museum explains typhoid this way.

316
00:14:42,615 --> 00:14:45,381
It spreads through contaminated food or water.

317
00:14:45,381 --> 00:14:48,326
In a crowded camp, anything that fouled the water

318
00:14:48,326 --> 00:14:50,009
could reach hundreds of men.

319
00:14:50,459 --> 00:14:51,715
So why did camps kill?

320
00:14:51,715 --> 00:14:54,112
The museum points to the places men lived.

321
00:14:54,112 --> 00:14:55,881
Improper placement of latrines.

322
00:14:55,881 --> 00:14:57,935
Food and water that carried typhoid.

323
00:14:58,385 --> 00:15:01,420
A latrine in the wrong place could foul the water a

324
00:15:01,420 --> 00:15:02,431
camp depended on.

325
00:15:02,431 --> 00:15:05,525
Water came from wells and rivers, and the army drank

326
00:15:05,525 --> 00:15:06,358
what it found.

327
00:15:06,808 --> 00:15:08,917
Surgeons asked for cleanliness.

328
00:15:08,917 --> 00:15:11,842
Officers and soldiers largely ignored them.

329
00:15:12,292 --> 00:15:14,658
And the dead were not always buried.

330
00:15:14,658 --> 00:15:17,877
After Antietam, a Union surgeon reported that the

331
00:15:17,877 --> 00:15:21,293
dead were almost wholly unburied, and the stench was

332
00:15:21,293 --> 00:15:23,264
such as to breed a pestilence.

333
00:15:23,714 --> 00:15:26,981
In all, the National Park Service says, about

334
00:15:26,981 --> 00:15:29,377
620,000 soldiers died in the war.

335
00:15:29,377 --> 00:15:32,136
More than any other American conflict.

336
00:15:32,586 --> 00:15:35,698
The Union army was, in a grim sense, the largest

337
00:15:35,698 --> 00:15:38,875
experiment in camp life the country had ever run.

338
00:15:38,875 --> 00:15:41,339
And its own doctors were taking notes.

339
00:15:41,789 --> 00:15:45,186
Pneumonia, measles, mumps, dysentery, typhoid.

340
00:15:45,186 --> 00:15:48,731
A soldier of 1862 could survive the bullets of a

341
00:15:48,731 --> 00:15:51,759
whole campaign and still never come home.

342
00:15:52,209 --> 00:15:53,673
But notes were not enough.

343
00:15:53,673 --> 00:15:56,321
Someone had to walk into the camps, look at the

344
00:15:56,321 --> 00:15:58,067
squalor, and tell the generals.

345
00:15:58,517 --> 00:16:00,857
It started with people who were not soldiers.

346
00:16:02,457 --> 00:16:06,209
On June 13, 1861, President Abraham Lincoln signed

347
00:16:06,209 --> 00:16:09,585
an executive order creating the United States

348
00:16:09,585 --> 00:16:11,086
Sanitary Commission.

349
00:16:11,536 --> 00:16:13,438
It was a civilian organization.

350
00:16:13,438 --> 00:16:16,688
Its job was to push the army to take care of its men.

351
00:16:16,688 --> 00:16:18,774
Its general secretary was Olmsted.

352
00:16:19,224 --> 00:16:22,314
Olmsted and his colleagues had no power to give

353
00:16:22,314 --> 00:16:23,629
orders to a general.

354
00:16:23,629 --> 00:16:26,983
What they had was a name, a presidential signature,

355
00:16:26,983 --> 00:16:30,007
and a willingness to write down what they saw.

356
00:16:30,457 --> 00:16:32,522
Its first weapon was the inspection.

357
00:16:32,522 --> 00:16:35,448
Inspectors walked through camps with a notebook and

358
00:16:35,448 --> 00:16:36,366
a hard question.

359
00:16:36,366 --> 00:16:37,628
How do these men live?

360
00:16:38,078 --> 00:16:43,234
By the war's end, they had made 1,482 camp inspections.

361
00:16:43,684 --> 00:16:46,142
What they found was often abject squalor.

362
00:16:46,142 --> 00:16:49,078
And the National Park Service notes that soldiers

363
00:16:49,078 --> 00:16:50,936
disregarded the sanitary rules.

364
00:16:51,386 --> 00:16:53,881
So the Commission did more than inspect.

365
00:16:53,881 --> 00:16:55,129
It treated the sick.

366
00:16:55,129 --> 00:16:58,185
In the Peninsula Campaign alone, upwards of 8,000

367
00:16:58,185 --> 00:16:59,245
sick and wounded.

368
00:16:59,695 --> 00:17:03,800
It ran on local aid societies, supplies and cash.

369
00:17:03,800 --> 00:17:05,811
286 local aid societies.

370
00:17:05,811 --> 00:17:07,821
$15 million in supplies.

371
00:17:07,821 --> 00:17:09,413
$5 million in cash.

372
00:17:09,863 --> 00:17:10,754
Money mattered.

373
00:17:10,754 --> 00:17:13,841
A wounded or sick man still had to be moved from the

374
00:17:13,841 --> 00:17:14,969
camp to a hospital.

375
00:17:14,969 --> 00:17:17,878
Trains and ships were how an army moved its sick.

376
00:17:18,328 --> 00:17:21,058
It funded hospital trains and hospital ships.

377
00:17:21,058 --> 00:17:23,364
It held sanitary fairs to raise money.

378
00:17:23,814 --> 00:17:26,624
And, the National Museum of Health and Medicine

379
00:17:26,624 --> 00:17:29,195
says, it pressured the army to order better

380
00:17:29,195 --> 00:17:31,766
sanitation, better hygiene and better food.

381
00:17:32,216 --> 00:17:34,736
We cannot put a number on the lives it saved.

382
00:17:34,736 --> 00:17:37,648
The sources record inspections, supplies and orders.

383
00:17:37,648 --> 00:17:40,169
They do not give a count of deaths prevented.

384
00:17:40,619 --> 00:17:41,737
It could argue.

385
00:17:41,737 --> 00:17:42,856
It could shame.

386
00:17:42,856 --> 00:17:46,733
It could not always make a regiment dig a latrine in

387
00:17:46,733 --> 00:17:47,926
the right place.

388
00:17:48,376 --> 00:17:50,636
Here is what makes the story strange.

389
00:17:50,636 --> 00:17:53,751
The cause of camp fevers was still argued over, and

390
00:17:53,751 --> 00:17:55,584
the proof was not yet in hand.

391
00:17:56,034 --> 00:17:57,343
They could see the dirt.

392
00:17:57,343 --> 00:17:59,962
They could see the sinks and the standing water.

393
00:17:59,962 --> 00:18:02,527
They could not yet see the road that ran from a

394
00:18:02,527 --> 00:18:03,946
latrine to a cup of water.

395
00:18:04,396 --> 00:18:07,068
Today, the chain is easy to draw.

396
00:18:07,068 --> 00:18:08,849
Latrine, water, mouth.

397
00:18:08,849 --> 00:18:12,979
In 1863, it was a hunch, argued in reports, against

398
00:18:12,979 --> 00:18:14,842
a war that kept moving.

399
00:18:15,292 --> 00:18:17,079
Cleaner camps may have helped.

400
00:18:17,079 --> 00:18:19,938
But cleaning without understanding is guesswork.

401
00:18:19,938 --> 00:18:22,917
The proof would have to be built, case by case, in

402
00:18:22,917 --> 00:18:24,048
someone's notebook.

403
00:18:24,498 --> 00:18:26,863
That proof would come in another war.

404
00:18:26,863 --> 00:18:30,315
In 1898, American camps would fill with typhoid again.

405
00:18:30,315 --> 00:18:33,510
And this time, an Army doctor would walk the camps

406
00:18:33,510 --> 00:18:35,044
and trace the sick list.

407
00:18:36,044 --> 00:18:37,872
It started with a question, in a letter.

408
00:18:39,472 --> 00:18:42,186
In 1898, the United States fought Spain.

409
00:18:42,186 --> 00:18:45,512
American troops stayed on in Cuba, and they met a

410
00:18:45,512 --> 00:18:46,801
second enemy there.

411
00:18:46,801 --> 00:18:49,923
PBS counts more than 2,000 soldiers who caught

412
00:18:49,923 --> 00:18:51,959
yellow fever in that campaign.

413
00:18:52,409 --> 00:18:55,547
In May 1900, Surgeon General George Sternberg named

414
00:18:55,547 --> 00:18:57,147
a Yellow Fever Commission.

415
00:18:57,147 --> 00:19:00,040
Its job was to find out how the disease spread.

416
00:19:00,040 --> 00:19:02,748
Its work would be done in Cuba, near Havana.

417
00:19:03,198 --> 00:19:04,716
Major Walter Reed led it.

418
00:19:05,166 --> 00:19:06,920
With him were three doctors:

419
00:19:06,920 --> 00:19:10,240
James Carroll, Aristides Agramonte, and Jesse Lazear.

420
00:19:10,690 --> 00:19:13,317
The idea they had to test was not new.

421
00:19:13,317 --> 00:19:16,705
A Cuban physician, Carlos Finlay, had proposed in

422
00:19:16,705 --> 00:19:19,609
1881 that a mosquito carried yellow fever.

423
00:19:20,059 --> 00:19:22,365
For almost twenty years, it stayed a theory.

424
00:19:22,815 --> 00:19:24,964
So the commission had to test it.

425
00:19:24,964 --> 00:19:27,047
The tests used human volunteers.

426
00:19:27,047 --> 00:19:29,977
Weeks later, Reed would write the question to

427
00:19:29,977 --> 00:19:32,647
Carroll in a letter, in five plain words.

428
00:19:33,097 --> 00:19:34,766
It did not work at first.

429
00:19:34,766 --> 00:19:38,038
In August, at Camp Columbia, the commission ran 9

430
00:19:38,038 --> 00:19:41,109
mosquito trials between the 11th and the 25th.

431
00:19:41,109 --> 00:19:42,645
Every one was negative.

432
00:19:43,095 --> 00:19:43,663
Then Dr.

433
00:19:43,663 --> 00:19:47,147
Carroll fell ill with yellow fever, and so did an

434
00:19:47,147 --> 00:19:48,071
Army private.

435
00:19:48,521 --> 00:19:48,859
Dr.

436
00:19:48,859 --> 00:19:51,109
Lazear fell ill too.

437
00:19:51,109 --> 00:19:55,722
On September 25, 1900, Jesse Lazear died.

438
00:19:56,172 --> 00:19:59,391
The University of Virginia's exhibit says his death

439
00:19:59,391 --> 00:20:02,357
was very likely from an experimental infection.

440
00:20:02,357 --> 00:20:05,450
The camp the commission built that winter carried

441
00:20:05,450 --> 00:20:06,018
his name.

442
00:20:06,468 --> 00:20:08,630
Camp Lazear opened on November 20.

443
00:20:08,630 --> 00:20:11,682
The men who came to it were volunteers, and they

444
00:20:11,682 --> 00:20:13,717
signed consent agreements first.

445
00:20:14,167 --> 00:20:17,584
The volunteers included American soldiers, Spanish

446
00:20:17,584 --> 00:20:21,069
volunteers, and one American civilian, who declined

447
00:20:21,069 --> 00:20:21,616
payment.

448
00:20:21,616 --> 00:20:24,828
Their names are in the University of Virginia's

449
00:20:24,828 --> 00:20:27,767
exhibit, and they deserve to be read there.

450
00:20:28,217 --> 00:20:31,373
PBS reports volunteers were offered $100, and

451
00:20:31,373 --> 00:20:34,178
another $100 if they caught the disease.

452
00:20:34,178 --> 00:20:37,545
The risk was real, and one of the commission had

453
00:20:37,545 --> 00:20:38,246
just died.

454
00:20:38,696 --> 00:20:41,982
At Camp Lazear, the first mosquito bites on four

455
00:20:41,982 --> 00:20:44,994
volunteers, over two weeks, changed nothing.

456
00:20:44,994 --> 00:20:48,143
Then, in December, one volunteer fell ill with

457
00:20:48,143 --> 00:20:51,497
yellow fever, and three more in rapid succession.

458
00:20:51,947 --> 00:20:54,794
Other volunteers lived in a building full of

459
00:20:54,794 --> 00:20:57,834
infected clothing, to test whether the sickness

460
00:20:57,834 --> 00:21:00,616
passed from person to person through cloth.

461
00:21:00,616 --> 00:21:03,139
7 men took part in three 20-day trials.

462
00:21:03,139 --> 00:21:04,110
None caught it.

463
00:21:04,560 --> 00:21:07,490
A third test used injected blood from patients.

464
00:21:07,490 --> 00:21:10,046
3 of 4 volunteers developed yellow fever.

465
00:21:10,046 --> 00:21:13,101
The commission also showed that a mosquito needed

466
00:21:13,101 --> 00:21:16,468
time after biting before it could pass the disease on.

467
00:21:16,918 --> 00:21:20,454
In all, the University of Virginia's account counts

468
00:21:20,454 --> 00:21:23,227
14 cases from mosquitoes and injections.

469
00:21:23,227 --> 00:21:25,169
All 14 volunteers recovered.

470
00:21:25,619 --> 00:21:27,650
The exhibit's verdict is plain.

471
00:21:27,650 --> 00:21:30,927
The commission had demonstrated, irrefutably, that

472
00:21:30,927 --> 00:21:33,156
the mosquito carried yellow fever.

473
00:21:33,606 --> 00:21:36,251
That finding came from four doctors, and from

474
00:21:36,251 --> 00:21:39,660
volunteers who took a risk that history should not forget.

475
00:21:40,110 --> 00:21:42,943
Now the knowledge could be used.

476
00:21:42,943 --> 00:21:47,282
From 1890 to 1900, Havana had averaged 462 yellow

477
00:21:47,282 --> 00:21:49,053
fever deaths a year.

478
00:21:49,503 --> 00:21:53,820
In January and February of 1901, the city counted 12.

479
00:21:53,820 --> 00:21:57,811
That year, the Army began two mosquito campaigns.

480
00:21:57,811 --> 00:22:01,720
The last death under American occupation came on

481
00:22:01,720 --> 00:22:03,268
September 28, 1901.

482
00:22:03,718 --> 00:22:06,236
The campaign was led by William Gorgas.

483
00:22:06,236 --> 00:22:09,271
According to the James Lind Library, it used 20

484
00:22:09,271 --> 00:22:12,242
districts, sanitation teams, and sealed houses

485
00:22:12,242 --> 00:22:13,856
fumigated with pyrethrum.

486
00:22:14,306 --> 00:22:18,000
By January 1903, a report said Cuba was free of

487
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:19,022
yellow fever.

488
00:22:19,022 --> 00:22:23,345
Decades later, mosquito control still looked like this:

489
00:22:23,345 --> 00:22:27,668
a Public Health Service film from Tampa, Florida, 1945.

490
00:22:28,118 --> 00:22:29,884
The work was patient and plain.

491
00:22:29,884 --> 00:22:32,676
Inspectors lifted covers, tipped out buckets, and

492
00:22:32,676 --> 00:22:35,524
looked for standing water where the mosquito could

493
00:22:35,524 --> 00:22:36,037
lay eggs.

494
00:22:36,487 --> 00:22:39,379
This is the mosquito itself, Aedes aegypti, in a

495
00:22:39,379 --> 00:22:41,789
training film made for the same service.

496
00:22:41,789 --> 00:22:44,500
The larvae live in the water we leave behind.

497
00:22:44,950 --> 00:22:48,594
The University of Virginia's exhibit puts it simply:

498
00:22:48,594 --> 00:22:52,168
after this work, yellow fever was largely conquered

499
00:22:52,168 --> 00:22:53,990
in the Western Hemisphere.

500
00:22:54,440 --> 00:22:56,292
The mosquito was one enemy.

501
00:22:56,292 --> 00:22:59,449
But in the camps of 1898, another killer moved

502
00:22:59,449 --> 00:23:01,576
through hands, water and flies.

503
00:23:01,576 --> 00:23:05,213
The Army was about to try a different weapon: a shot.

504
00:23:06,413 --> 00:23:07,859
It started with an idea:

505
00:23:07,859 --> 00:23:09,908
stop the disease before it starts.

506
00:23:11,507 --> 00:23:16,793
In the Spanish-American War, the Army's own history

507
00:23:16,793 --> 00:23:21,561
counts 20,926 typhoid cases, and 2,192 deaths.

508
00:23:22,011 --> 00:23:24,819
An Army board went through the records of 92

509
00:23:24,819 --> 00:23:26,926
regiments to learn how it spread.

510
00:23:26,926 --> 00:23:29,990
Its report ran to nearly 3,000 manuscript pages.

511
00:23:29,990 --> 00:23:33,054
It was written by Reed, Vaughan and Shakespeare.

512
00:23:33,504 --> 00:23:37,123
The board's report said the disease spread from man

513
00:23:37,123 --> 00:23:40,031
to man, through flies, water and contact.

514
00:23:40,031 --> 00:23:42,160
In one of its blunt sentences:

515
00:23:42,160 --> 00:23:45,211
Man himself is the most active agent in the

516
00:23:45,211 --> 00:23:47,339
dissemination of this disease.

517
00:23:47,789 --> 00:23:51,147
The answer would have to reach the men themselves.

518
00:23:51,147 --> 00:23:54,504
In 1909, the Army began offering a typhoid vaccine

519
00:23:54,504 --> 00:23:56,183
to soldiers who chose it.

520
00:23:56,183 --> 00:23:59,608
The National Museum of Health and Medicine says 830

521
00:23:59,608 --> 00:24:01,421
volunteers were vaccinated.

522
00:24:01,871 --> 00:24:05,307
The museum calls the 1909 effort the Army's first

523
00:24:05,307 --> 00:24:08,112
large-scale typhoid vaccination program.

524
00:24:08,112 --> 00:24:11,688
The vaccine was still new, and the choice was still

525
00:24:11,688 --> 00:24:12,389
voluntary.

526
00:24:12,839 --> 00:24:16,176
In early 1911, it became required for troops sent to

527
00:24:16,176 --> 00:24:17,459
the southern border.

528
00:24:17,459 --> 00:24:20,795
Later that year, it became mandatory for everyone in

529
00:24:20,795 --> 00:24:21,565
the service.

530
00:24:22,015 --> 00:24:25,250
Then came a striking comparison.

531
00:24:25,250 --> 00:24:30,102
At San Antonio in 1911, a vaccinated division of

532
00:24:30,102 --> 00:24:31,214
12,800 men.

533
00:24:31,214 --> 00:24:36,268
In 1898, a division of 10,700, before the vaccine.

534
00:24:36,718 --> 00:24:41,946
The 1898 division recorded 2,693 cases and 248 deaths.

535
00:24:41,946 --> 00:24:46,981
The vaccinated division had one case, and no deaths.

536
00:24:47,431 --> 00:24:50,608
Those were different years and different places.

537
00:24:50,608 --> 00:24:52,593
It was not a controlled trial.

538
00:24:52,593 --> 00:24:55,770
But a closer comparison came in 1917, inside one

539
00:24:55,770 --> 00:24:57,623
post, at Schofield Barracks.

540
00:24:58,073 --> 00:25:03,747
Among 4,087 vaccinated men, 55 fell ill and four died.

541
00:25:03,747 --> 00:25:09,000
Among 812 who were not vaccinated, 45 fell ill and

542
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:10,156
seven died.

543
00:25:10,606 --> 00:25:14,940
In the World War, the Army recorded 1,529 first

544
00:25:14,940 --> 00:25:19,365
admissions for typhoid fever, a rate of 0.37 per

545
00:25:19,365 --> 00:25:21,209
thousand men a year.

546
00:25:21,659 --> 00:25:25,559
In the Spanish-American War, the rate had been

547
00:25:25,559 --> 00:25:27,847
141.59 per thousand a year.

548
00:25:28,297 --> 00:25:32,957
In the Army, the typhoid death rate in the World War

549
00:25:32,957 --> 00:25:36,452
was about 0.05 per thousand men a year.

550
00:25:36,452 --> 00:25:41,022
For civilian men aged 20 to 34, it was about 0.117.

551
00:25:41,472 --> 00:25:45,077
One scientist estimated that at the Spanish-American

552
00:25:45,077 --> 00:25:48,612
War's rates, the World War army would have suffered

553
00:25:48,612 --> 00:25:49,999
about 500,000 cases.

554
00:25:49,999 --> 00:25:52,286
That is an estimate, not a count.

555
00:25:52,736 --> 00:25:55,063
The shot was not the only defense.

556
00:25:55,063 --> 00:25:58,485
The camps of 1918 also purified their water, which

557
00:25:58,485 --> 00:26:01,976
is what you see here, and kept flies away from food

558
00:26:01,976 --> 00:26:02,797
and garbage.

559
00:26:03,247 --> 00:26:05,722
Here is the filter room of the same plant.

560
00:26:05,722 --> 00:26:08,610
Clean water, closed garbage cans, and a needle in

561
00:26:08,610 --> 00:26:10,201
the arm all worked at once.

562
00:26:10,651 --> 00:26:12,627
An association is not a cause.

563
00:26:12,627 --> 00:26:16,381
But across these records, the line goes in one direction.

564
00:26:16,381 --> 00:26:19,344
Where soldiers were vaccinated, typhoid fell.

565
00:26:19,794 --> 00:26:23,195
A typhoid shot was routine at Camp Sherman, Ohio.

566
00:26:23,195 --> 00:26:26,319
An intertitle in its Signal Corps film reads:

567
00:26:26,319 --> 00:26:29,928
Inoculation prevents typhoid fever and other serious

568
00:26:29,928 --> 00:26:30,552
diseases.

569
00:26:31,002 --> 00:26:34,337
But in 1918, a different disease was about to reach

570
00:26:34,337 --> 00:26:37,672
the camps, one that no shot of that day could stop.

571
00:26:38,872 --> 00:26:41,280
It started in March, at a camp in Kansas.

572
00:26:42,880 --> 00:26:47,549
On March 4, 1918, the first cases were reported at

573
00:26:47,549 --> 00:26:49,510
Camp Funston, Kansas.

574
00:26:49,510 --> 00:26:54,833
Within three weeks, 1,100 of its 56,222 troops were sick.

575
00:26:55,283 --> 00:26:58,625
Camps like this gathered tens of thousands of men in

576
00:26:58,625 --> 00:26:59,267
one place.

577
00:26:59,267 --> 00:27:02,545
This is Camp Sherman, in Ohio, a different camp, in

578
00:27:02,545 --> 00:27:03,381
the same war.

579
00:27:03,381 --> 00:27:04,859
Funston was 56,000 men.

580
00:27:05,309 --> 00:27:07,889
Men ate, slept, drilled and lined up together, by

581
00:27:07,889 --> 00:27:08,574
the thousand.

582
00:27:08,574 --> 00:27:11,049
Every one of them would soon be sent somewhere.

583
00:27:11,499 --> 00:27:12,619
Many went to sea.

584
00:27:12,619 --> 00:27:15,651
The National Guard Bureau says the crossing to

585
00:27:15,651 --> 00:27:18,881
Europe took 10 to 14 days, and many soldiers fell

586
00:27:18,881 --> 00:27:19,936
sick on the way.

587
00:27:20,386 --> 00:27:21,409
From the S.S.

588
00:27:21,409 --> 00:27:25,347
Grant, a sailor on another ship counted 15 burials

589
00:27:25,347 --> 00:27:27,159
at sea in a single day.

590
00:27:27,609 --> 00:27:32,011
In Europe in 1918, the National Guard Bureau says

591
00:27:32,011 --> 00:27:36,413
about 340,000 American soldiers were hospitalized

592
00:27:36,413 --> 00:27:37,761
with influenza.

593
00:27:37,761 --> 00:27:42,253
About 227,000 were hospitalized for battle wounds.

594
00:27:42,703 --> 00:27:46,122
The epidemic peaked in the Army in the middle of October.

595
00:27:46,122 --> 00:27:49,301
By the Navy's count, its own deaths were about 5,000.

596
00:27:49,751 --> 00:27:53,226
The Navy's history says over a million Army men were

597
00:27:53,226 --> 00:27:56,033
hospitalized with influenza and pneumonia.

598
00:27:56,033 --> 00:27:59,307
After adjusting for age and sex, Army death rates

599
00:27:59,307 --> 00:28:02,582
ran about one fourth higher than among civilians.

600
00:28:03,032 --> 00:28:05,922
In March and April, the Navy's history says, Camp

601
00:28:05,922 --> 00:28:08,518
Funston went through three waves of illness.

602
00:28:08,518 --> 00:28:11,349
Men who got well were still part of an army that

603
00:28:11,349 --> 00:28:12,057
kept moving.

604
00:28:12,507 --> 00:28:16,510
The National Museum of Health and Medicine says the

605
00:28:16,510 --> 00:28:20,435
1918 pandemic killed an estimated 21 to 50 million

606
00:28:20,435 --> 00:28:21,770
people worldwide.

607
00:28:21,770 --> 00:28:25,459
The soldiers' share is small by comparison, and

608
00:28:25,459 --> 00:28:26,637
still enormous.

609
00:28:27,087 --> 00:28:29,116
The flu reached cities too.

610
00:28:29,116 --> 00:28:32,274
This is a parade in San Francisco in 1918,

611
00:28:32,274 --> 00:28:34,604
celebrating the end of the war.

612
00:28:34,604 --> 00:28:38,814
The archive says the paraders are all wearing flu masks.

613
00:28:39,264 --> 00:28:41,752
So how many soldiers died of influenza?

614
00:28:41,752 --> 00:28:43,346
The sources do not agree.

615
00:28:43,346 --> 00:28:45,004
We will show you each one.

616
00:28:45,454 --> 00:28:49,681
The National Guard Bureau says about 45,000 American

617
00:28:49,681 --> 00:28:53,826
soldiers died of influenza and related pneumonia by

618
00:28:53,826 --> 00:28:55,126
the end of 1918.

619
00:28:55,576 --> 00:28:59,142
The Navy's history says more than 44,000.

620
00:28:59,142 --> 00:29:03,403
And the University of Kansas Medical Center cites

621
00:29:03,403 --> 00:29:07,752
24,664, adding that most of those deaths came from

622
00:29:07,752 --> 00:29:10,622
complicating bacterial pneumonia.

623
00:29:11,072 --> 00:29:13,774
That is why we lean on the World War numbers.

624
00:29:13,774 --> 00:29:16,476
The Congressional Research Service warns that

625
00:29:16,476 --> 00:29:19,358
casualty records before it are often incomplete.

626
00:29:19,808 --> 00:29:24,507
Now the Army's own totals for the whole war, from

627
00:29:24,507 --> 00:29:27,480
its Center of Military History.

628
00:29:27,480 --> 00:29:29,782
37,171 killed in action.

629
00:29:29,782 --> 00:29:31,892
12,934 died of wounds.

630
00:29:32,342 --> 00:29:35,771
Together, that is 50,105 battle deaths.

631
00:29:35,771 --> 00:29:39,727
But the Army counts 55,868 non-battle deaths.

632
00:29:39,727 --> 00:29:44,210
More men died of everything else than of the enemy.

633
00:29:44,660 --> 00:29:48,052
Non-battle includes accidents and other causes, and

634
00:29:48,052 --> 00:29:51,776
the Army's fact sheet gives no figure for disease alone.

635
00:29:51,776 --> 00:29:53,505
So we will not invent one.

636
00:29:53,505 --> 00:29:56,431
But the influenza deaths are in that column.

637
00:29:56,881 --> 00:30:01,391
Across all the services, the Congressional Research

638
00:30:01,391 --> 00:30:05,547
Service counts 53,402 battle deaths, and 63,114

639
00:30:05,547 --> 00:30:06,697
other deaths.

640
00:30:07,147 --> 00:30:10,110
For the World War, non-battle deaths outnumbered

641
00:30:10,110 --> 00:30:13,320
battle deaths in the Army, and influenza was a large

642
00:30:13,320 --> 00:30:13,999
part of it.

643
00:30:14,449 --> 00:30:17,322
Twenty years later, in the next world war, the

644
00:30:17,322 --> 00:30:18,196
pattern broke.

645
00:30:18,196 --> 00:30:20,382
Disease still filled the hospitals.

646
00:30:20,382 --> 00:30:23,568
But it no longer killed more soldiers than bullets.

647
00:30:24,768 --> 00:30:27,622
It started with a number that breaks this video's title.

648
00:30:29,222 --> 00:30:33,920
Here is the honest answer for World War II.

649
00:30:33,920 --> 00:30:38,182
The Army counted 229,823 battle deaths.

650
00:30:38,182 --> 00:30:41,241
Deaths from disease: 14,904.

651
00:30:41,691 --> 00:30:45,028
The Army's own statistics volume says that battle

652
00:30:45,028 --> 00:30:48,502
deaths exceeded all non-battle deaths by a ratio of

653
00:30:48,502 --> 00:30:49,387
three to one.

654
00:30:49,387 --> 00:30:52,384
For deaths, in this war, the title is wrong.

655
00:30:52,834 --> 00:30:55,532
But there is more than one way to count a war.

656
00:30:55,532 --> 00:30:58,524
Count the medical admissions, and the picture turns

657
00:30:58,524 --> 00:30:59,228
upside down.

658
00:30:59,678 --> 00:31:05,909
From 1942 to 1945, the Army recorded 14,998,369

659
00:31:05,909 --> 00:31:11,743
medical admissions for disease, in hospital,

660
00:31:11,743 --> 00:31:14,660
infirmary or quarters.

661
00:31:14,660 --> 00:31:18,240
For battle wounds: 723,560.

662
00:31:18,690 --> 00:31:20,891
Disease did not win the count of the dead.

663
00:31:20,891 --> 00:31:22,411
It won the count of the sick.

664
00:31:22,411 --> 00:31:24,874
And in the South Pacific, the name that came up

665
00:31:24,874 --> 00:31:26,342
again and again was malaria.

666
00:31:26,792 --> 00:31:27,367
Malaria.

667
00:31:27,367 --> 00:31:30,821
About 500,000 hospital admissions in the Army in

668
00:31:30,821 --> 00:31:34,563
this war, according to the Army's own history of its

669
00:31:34,563 --> 00:31:36,002
preventive medicine.

670
00:31:36,452 --> 00:31:39,230
It had not always been so bad.

671
00:31:39,230 --> 00:31:43,581
In 1900, Army malaria admissions ran at 130 per

672
00:31:43,581 --> 00:31:45,896
thousand soldiers a year.

673
00:31:45,896 --> 00:31:47,655
By 1938, about 4.6.

674
00:31:48,105 --> 00:31:50,394
Then the Army went to the islands.

675
00:31:50,394 --> 00:31:53,894
The Army Heritage Center says that between 60 and 65

676
00:31:53,894 --> 00:31:57,126
percent of soldiers serving in the South Pacific

677
00:31:57,126 --> 00:31:59,684
reported having malaria at some point.

678
00:32:00,134 --> 00:32:04,060
On Guadalcanal, from November 1942 to February 1943,

679
00:32:04,060 --> 00:32:07,986
malaria made up about half of the disease casualties

680
00:32:07,986 --> 00:32:12,365
at three field hospitals run by one Army medical regiment.

681
00:32:12,815 --> 00:32:16,990
In 1943 alone, there were more than 100,000 malaria

682
00:32:16,990 --> 00:32:20,347
cases in the Southwest and South Pacific.

683
00:32:20,347 --> 00:32:24,440
And 3,334 malaria patients were evacuated from the

684
00:32:24,440 --> 00:32:27,305
South Pacific to the United States.

685
00:32:27,755 --> 00:32:30,718
One medical officer put the danger plainly:

686
00:32:30,718 --> 00:32:33,818
if the malaria situation is not brought under

687
00:32:33,818 --> 00:32:37,194
control, the efficiency of the whole Army will be

688
00:32:37,194 --> 00:32:38,366
greatly impaired.

689
00:32:38,815 --> 00:32:40,462
Planners did the arithmetic.

690
00:32:40,462 --> 00:32:43,462
According to the military's health journal, keeping

691
00:32:43,462 --> 00:32:46,343
one division in contact with the enemy could take

692
00:32:46,343 --> 00:32:49,696
three divisions, and as many as five, because of malaria.

693
00:32:50,145 --> 00:32:51,605
Bataan, early 1942.

694
00:32:51,605 --> 00:32:55,446
There had been little or no malaria control before

695
00:32:55,446 --> 00:32:56,060
the war.

696
00:32:56,060 --> 00:32:59,593
Roughly 24,000 of 75,000 American and Filipino

697
00:32:59,593 --> 00:33:02,128
defenders were suffering from it.

698
00:33:02,578 --> 00:33:05,964
In early March, about 500 men a day were admitted

699
00:33:05,964 --> 00:33:07,208
for malaria alone.

700
00:33:07,208 --> 00:33:10,455
By month's end, hospital admissions were nearly

701
00:33:10,455 --> 00:33:11,285
1,000 a day.

702
00:33:11,285 --> 00:33:14,256
The Army's history says 75 to 80 percent of

703
00:33:14,256 --> 00:33:15,776
front-line men had it.

704
00:33:16,226 --> 00:33:19,507
So the Army built an answer, and it was mostly people.

705
00:33:19,507 --> 00:33:22,241
Survey units found where the mosquitoes bred.

706
00:33:22,241 --> 00:33:24,793
Control units drained, cleared, and oiled.

707
00:33:24,793 --> 00:33:27,588
They trained at places like the Army School of

708
00:33:27,588 --> 00:33:29,896
Malariology, in the Panama Canal Zone.

709
00:33:30,346 --> 00:33:33,634
In January 1943, the first group was ready:

710
00:33:33,634 --> 00:33:36,921
six survey units and fifteen control units.

711
00:33:36,921 --> 00:33:40,437
By October, the Surgeon General recommended 50

712
00:33:40,437 --> 00:33:43,113
survey units and 105 control units.

713
00:33:43,563 --> 00:33:45,437
Drainage was the simplest weapon.

714
00:33:45,437 --> 00:33:48,333
Take away the standing water, and you take away the

715
00:33:48,333 --> 00:33:48,788
nursery.

716
00:33:49,238 --> 00:33:52,081
A Navy film from the time shows the work.

717
00:33:52,081 --> 00:33:54,369
A laboratory bench. A microscope.

718
00:33:54,369 --> 00:33:55,479
Larvae in a jar.

719
00:33:55,929 --> 00:33:58,792
And inspectors in the field, dipping larvae out of

720
00:33:58,792 --> 00:34:00,453
puddles and drainage ditches.

721
00:34:00,903 --> 00:34:01,969
Then came DDT.

722
00:34:01,969 --> 00:34:05,546
The Army's malaria history says the first field

723
00:34:05,546 --> 00:34:09,351
trial of a DDT residual spray took place in Italy,

724
00:34:09,351 --> 00:34:09,960
in 1944.

725
00:34:09,960 --> 00:34:13,766
The same history shows it being applied by hand in

726
00:34:13,766 --> 00:34:14,831
Army barracks.

727
00:34:15,281 --> 00:34:16,572
An Army film, DDT:

728
00:34:16,572 --> 00:34:20,231
Weapon Against Disease, shows a soldier with a hand

729
00:34:20,231 --> 00:34:23,244
sprayer on a river bank, in clouds of fog.

730
00:34:23,694 --> 00:34:25,490
Posters boiled the rules down.

731
00:34:25,490 --> 00:34:28,543
Keep covered from sundown to sunrise, one said, and

732
00:34:28,543 --> 00:34:29,680
use your repellent.

733
00:34:30,130 --> 00:34:32,144
At night, a hammock, and a net over it.

734
00:34:32,594 --> 00:34:33,749
Then the tablets.

735
00:34:33,749 --> 00:34:36,877
The Army's history says that enforced, regular

736
00:34:36,877 --> 00:34:40,276
Atabrine dosing, together with malaria discipline,

737
00:34:40,276 --> 00:34:43,199
allowed forces to fight in malarious areas.

738
00:34:43,649 --> 00:34:44,604
It had a cost.

739
00:34:44,604 --> 00:34:47,401
Atabrine turned the skin a sallow yellow.

740
00:34:47,401 --> 00:34:49,311
Some men refused to take it.

741
00:34:49,311 --> 00:34:52,654
A man who stopped, the Heritage Center notes, was

742
00:34:52,654 --> 00:34:54,155
virtually unprotected.

743
00:34:54,605 --> 00:34:57,692
So malaria became a matter of discipline.

744
00:34:57,692 --> 00:35:00,026
A poster of 1943 told soldiers:

745
00:35:00,026 --> 00:35:01,306
don't be a dummy.

746
00:35:01,756 --> 00:35:05,079
Army manuals of the period also showed how mosquito

747
00:35:05,079 --> 00:35:08,402
control crews could treat breeding areas, even from

748
00:35:08,402 --> 00:35:08,923
the air.

749
00:35:09,373 --> 00:35:13,605
Malaria followed the Army to Sicily.

750
00:35:13,605 --> 00:35:19,365
Between July 9 and September 10, 1943, 21,482 men

751
00:35:19,365 --> 00:35:23,009
were hospitalized with malaria.

752
00:35:23,009 --> 00:35:25,360
17,375, with wounds.

753
00:35:25,810 --> 00:35:29,124
A Navy malaria film shows the harbor and streets of

754
00:35:29,124 --> 00:35:30,293
Licata, in Sicily.

755
00:35:30,743 --> 00:35:33,869
In India, near Calcutta, the Army's manual shows a

756
00:35:33,869 --> 00:35:36,808
tank full of water plants, a breeding place for

757
00:35:36,808 --> 00:35:38,997
mosquitoes right beside a campsite.

758
00:35:39,447 --> 00:35:41,163
None of this was glamorous.

759
00:35:41,163 --> 00:35:43,451
Survey, drain, oil, spray, cover up.

760
00:35:43,451 --> 00:35:46,374
But the Army's history credits it with letting

761
00:35:46,374 --> 00:35:49,807
forces fight in places where malaria had been winning.

762
00:35:50,257 --> 00:35:53,157
And in the Mediterranean, a different insect was

763
00:35:53,157 --> 00:35:56,118
about to start a different epidemic, in a city of

764
00:35:56,118 --> 00:35:57,145
crowded shelters.

765
00:35:57,595 --> 00:36:00,195
It started in September, in the city of Naples.

766
00:36:01,795 --> 00:36:03,704
Typhus is carried by lice.

767
00:36:03,704 --> 00:36:07,523
In September 1943, as Allied armies fought their way

768
00:36:07,523 --> 00:36:10,460
up Italy, cases were reported in Naples.

769
00:36:10,910 --> 00:36:13,602
A wartime report warned of the imminence of a

770
00:36:13,602 --> 00:36:14,619
serious epidemic.

771
00:36:15,069 --> 00:36:17,924
An American Typhus Commission took on the problem,

772
00:36:17,924 --> 00:36:19,866
and Fred Soper was assigned to it.

773
00:36:19,866 --> 00:36:22,493
Its field teams, eight of them, one hospital's

774
00:36:22,493 --> 00:36:23,920
history says, were small.

775
00:36:24,370 --> 00:36:26,363
The weapon was a louse powder.

776
00:36:26,363 --> 00:36:29,617
The Anglo-American account credits the pioneering

777
00:36:29,617 --> 00:36:30,348
use of DDT.

778
00:36:30,348 --> 00:36:33,204
In this photograph, an Italian woman uses a

779
00:36:33,204 --> 00:36:36,657
hand-operated dust gun to apply louse powder under a

780
00:36:36,657 --> 00:36:37,919
boy's shirt sleeve.

781
00:36:38,369 --> 00:36:41,302
In the Army's film, civilians stand in a long line

782
00:36:41,302 --> 00:36:43,766
while workers dust their sleeves and hair.

783
00:36:44,216 --> 00:36:47,461
People in the bomb shelters, one hospital's history

784
00:36:47,461 --> 00:36:51,024
says, were clamoring for the magic louse-killing powder.

785
00:36:51,474 --> 00:36:52,743
Machines sped it up.

786
00:36:52,743 --> 00:36:55,916
A gasoline-motor-driven applicator could dust many

787
00:36:55,916 --> 00:36:57,375
clothed people at once.

788
00:36:57,825 --> 00:37:00,586
Mass dusting began in mid-December.

789
00:37:00,586 --> 00:37:04,214
By January 10, 1944, the dusting stations were

790
00:37:04,214 --> 00:37:07,290
treating more than 50,000 people a day.

791
00:37:07,740 --> 00:37:09,515
New cases began to fall off.

792
00:37:09,515 --> 00:37:12,685
By late February, the problem was nearly resolved.

793
00:37:13,135 --> 00:37:16,711
In a test at a prison in Algiers, with about 1,000

794
00:37:16,711 --> 00:37:20,287
inmates, one powder called MYL killed lice faster.

795
00:37:20,287 --> 00:37:23,290
But the effects of DDT lasted much longer.

796
00:37:23,740 --> 00:37:27,317
That is the Anglo-American story, which one article

797
00:37:27,317 --> 00:37:30,613
from Oxford's history faculty calls triumphant.

798
00:37:30,613 --> 00:37:34,049
The author, Bailey, notes the epidemic is largely

799
00:37:34,049 --> 00:37:37,416
missing from Italian accounts, and may have left

800
00:37:37,416 --> 00:37:38,958
most people unscathed.

801
00:37:39,408 --> 00:37:41,321
What we can say is narrower.

802
00:37:41,321 --> 00:37:44,668
A powder, a plan, and tens of thousands of people

803
00:37:44,668 --> 00:37:45,966
lining up each day.

804
00:37:45,966 --> 00:37:48,494
By late February, the alarm was over.

805
00:37:48,944 --> 00:37:52,697
Those old lessons, water, waste, insects and shots,

806
00:37:52,697 --> 00:37:54,611
did not stay in the 1940s.

807
00:37:54,611 --> 00:37:57,040
So what does the military do now?

808
00:37:57,490 --> 00:37:58,597
It started with a share.

809
00:38:00,197 --> 00:38:03,740
The military's health journal, MSMR, notes that the

810
00:38:03,740 --> 00:38:07,213
share of deaths from disease and non-battle injury

811
00:38:07,213 --> 00:38:10,616
fell from about 60 percent in the Civil War to 20

812
00:38:10,616 --> 00:38:11,936
percent in Vietnam.

813
00:38:12,386 --> 00:38:16,547
The Congressional Research Service counts 47,434

814
00:38:16,547 --> 00:38:21,055
battle deaths in Vietnam, and 10,786 other deaths in

815
00:38:21,055 --> 00:38:22,096
the theater.

816
00:38:22,096 --> 00:38:26,344
Other deaths include accidents, not only disease.

817
00:38:26,794 --> 00:38:29,658
Disease did not disappear.

818
00:38:29,658 --> 00:38:34,835
In 1968, the CDC counted 2,598 imported malaria

819
00:38:34,835 --> 00:38:37,809
cases in the United States.

820
00:38:37,809 --> 00:38:40,453
2,444 came from Vietnam.

821
00:38:40,903 --> 00:38:43,660
In the early months of the Iraq War, the same

822
00:38:43,660 --> 00:38:46,785
journal notes, non-battle injuries were three times

823
00:38:46,785 --> 00:38:48,562
those caused by enemy action.

824
00:38:49,012 --> 00:38:51,648
Today, the Army Public Health Center has a

825
00:38:51,648 --> 00:38:54,660
Preventive Medicine Division that, in its words,

826
00:38:54,660 --> 00:38:57,797
routinely monitors military health system data for

827
00:38:57,797 --> 00:38:58,990
infectious disease.

828
00:38:59,440 --> 00:39:02,361
Reportable medical events come in through a system

829
00:39:02,361 --> 00:39:05,341
called the Disease Reporting Surveillance Internet,

830
00:39:05,341 --> 00:39:07,503
so the numbers are watched routinely.

831
00:39:07,953 --> 00:39:10,054
Water. Waste. Insects. Shots.

832
00:39:10,054 --> 00:39:13,676
Four old lessons that run through every chapter of

833
00:39:13,676 --> 00:39:14,472
this video.

834
00:39:14,922 --> 00:39:16,200
Start with water.

835
00:39:16,200 --> 00:39:20,108
An Army film from 1944 shows a purification station:

836
00:39:20,108 --> 00:39:23,865
barrels, a canvas bag, and a soldier drinking from

837
00:39:23,865 --> 00:39:24,767
his canteen.

838
00:39:25,217 --> 00:39:27,304
Insect control came home, too.

839
00:39:27,304 --> 00:39:30,573
A Public Health Service training film from 1945

840
00:39:30,573 --> 00:39:34,191
shows the larvae of Aedes aegypti, the mosquito that

841
00:39:34,191 --> 00:39:36,417
carries yellow fever and dengue.

842
00:39:36,867 --> 00:39:40,761
The Army's World War I sanitation volume shows crews

843
00:39:40,761 --> 00:39:44,431
oiling small pools with knapsack sprayers at Camp

844
00:39:44,431 --> 00:39:46,303
Meade, Maryland, in 1918.

845
00:39:46,753 --> 00:39:47,435
And shots.

846
00:39:47,435 --> 00:39:50,436
The Army's own advice, in that same article,

847
00:39:50,436 --> 00:39:54,119
includes a line from the oldest chapter of this story.

848
00:39:54,569 --> 00:39:57,954
The Army's article adds that the CDC recommends a

849
00:39:57,954 --> 00:40:01,408
yearly flu vaccine for people older than 6 months.

850
00:40:01,408 --> 00:40:04,240
That is the Army quoting the CDC, not us.

851
00:40:04,690 --> 00:40:08,621
On October 2, 2026, at Marine Corps Air Station

852
00:40:08,621 --> 00:40:13,138
Iwakuni, in Japan, a Navy clinic ran flu vaccinations.

853
00:40:13,588 --> 00:40:16,637
At Ramstein, in Germany, an immunizations clinic

854
00:40:16,637 --> 00:40:17,463
does one job:

855
00:40:17,463 --> 00:40:20,575
the paperwork, the vial, the needle, and then the

856
00:40:20,575 --> 00:40:21,845
next person in line.

857
00:40:22,295 --> 00:40:25,687
On October 26, at Camp Carroll, in South Korea, a

858
00:40:25,687 --> 00:40:28,940
flu drive filled a large hall with soldiers and

859
00:40:28,940 --> 00:40:31,640
staff, and a line of sleeves rolled up.

860
00:40:32,090 --> 00:40:33,815
The camps are different now.

861
00:40:33,815 --> 00:40:36,649
The enemy, in the end, is older than any army.

862
00:40:37,099 --> 00:40:40,291
So, did disease kill more soldiers than bullets?

863
00:40:40,291 --> 00:40:43,682
Four American wars say yes, if you count non-battle

864
00:40:43,682 --> 00:40:45,610
deaths against battle deaths.

865
00:40:45,610 --> 00:40:48,602
The Congressional Research Service warns that

866
00:40:48,602 --> 00:40:51,793
records before World War I are often incomplete.

867
00:40:52,243 --> 00:40:58,460
In the Mexican War, 1,733 died in battle, and 11,550

868
00:40:58,460 --> 00:41:00,372
of other causes.

869
00:41:00,822 --> 00:41:07,176
In the Civil War, on the Union side, 140,414 died in

870
00:41:07,176 --> 00:41:08,032
battle.

871
00:41:08,032 --> 00:41:11,575
224,097 died of other causes.

872
00:41:12,025 --> 00:41:15,940
In the Spanish–American War, 385.

873
00:41:15,940 --> 00:41:17,601
Against 2,061.

874
00:41:18,051 --> 00:41:22,135
In World War I, the Army counted 50,105 battle

875
00:41:22,135 --> 00:41:26,397
deaths, killed in action and died of wounds, and

876
00:41:26,397 --> 00:41:28,617
55,868 non-battle deaths.

877
00:41:28,617 --> 00:41:31,725
Influenza was a large part of that.

878
00:41:32,175 --> 00:41:35,505
Non-battle includes accidents, so it is not a pure

879
00:41:35,505 --> 00:41:36,637
count of disease.

880
00:41:36,637 --> 00:41:39,834
But in the camps of these wars, the records keep

881
00:41:39,834 --> 00:41:41,698
pointing at the same things:

882
00:41:41,698 --> 00:41:44,096
crowding, filthy water, and insects.

883
00:41:44,546 --> 00:41:48,557
World War II is where the pattern broke.

884
00:41:48,557 --> 00:41:53,772
Battle won the count of the dead, 229,823 to 14,904.

885
00:41:53,772 --> 00:41:58,184
Disease won the count of medical admissions.

886
00:41:58,634 --> 00:42:01,884
The change was not magic, and the records do not say

887
00:42:01,884 --> 00:42:03,696
how much each piece mattered.

888
00:42:03,696 --> 00:42:06,821
Survey units, clean water, drained ponds, powders,

889
00:42:06,821 --> 00:42:09,883
vaccines, and people willing to count and enforce

890
00:42:09,883 --> 00:42:11,133
were all part of it.

891
00:42:11,583 --> 00:42:14,100
Disease is a quieter enemy than a rifle.

892
00:42:14,100 --> 00:42:16,114
It never stopped being an enemy.

